Link

Byte layouts and derivations for link establishment. For what a link
is and why it exists, see upstream docs/source/understanding.rst,
section "Reticulum Link Establishment".

Vectors: test/linkrequest/, test/linkproof/, test/linkdata/

See doc/packet for the header. Only the payload and the derivations
are described here.


Constants

    ECPUBSIZE       64 bytes   RNS/Link.py:70#ECPUBSIZE, both public keys
    LINK_MTU_SIZE    3 bytes   RNS/Link.py:80#LINK_MTU_SIZE
    MTU_BYTEMASK  0x1fffff     RNS/Link.py:144#MTU_BYTEMASK
    MODE_BYTEMASK     0xe0     RNS/Link.py:145#MODE_BYTEMASK
    MODE_DEFAULT      0x01     RNS/Link.py:134#MODE_DEFAULT, AES-256-CBC

Only MODE_AES256_CBC is enabled at this pin. ENABLED_MODES holds that
one value, and signalling_bytes raises on any other, so no packet the
reference emits carries a different mode.

    RNS/Link.py:133#ENABLED_MODES, RNS/Link.py:148#signalling_bytes

The decoder is the other way round: mode_from_lr_packet returns the
three bits it read, whatever they are, and the refusal happens later.
So a request signalling a mode with no name here decodes, and mode
prints as two hex digits rather than a keyword.
test/linkrequest/unnamed-mode carries MODE_AES128_CBC, which the
reference defines, does not enable and will not emit.

    RNS/Link.py:174#MODE_BYTEMASK


Link request

Packet type 2, destination type single, context 0x00. The destination
hash is the hash of the destination being linked to. The payload is
not encrypted.

    RNS/Packet.py:193#LINKREQUEST

    offset  size  field
    0       32    initiator X25519 public key
    32      32    initiator Ed25519 public key
    64      3     signalling, optional

Both keys are drawn fresh for every link. The Ed25519 key is not the
initiator's identity key: a link is anonymous until the initiator
identifies over it.

    RNS/Link.py:284#X25519PrivateKey

Exactly two payload lengths are accepted, 64 and 67. Anything else is
dropped without further examination.

    RNS/Link.py:187#LINK_MTU_SIZE


Signalling

Three bytes, big endian, present only in the 67-byte form. The
reference always sends them.

    bit    width  field
    23-21  3      mode
    20-0   21     mtu

    RNS/Link.py:148#signalling_bytes

Absent, the mode decodes as MODE_DEFAULT, because mode_from_lr_packet
returns that value rather than nothing. The MTU has no such fallback
in the decoder: mtu_from_lr_packet returns nothing, and the receiver
keeps the MTU its own Link was constructed with, 500 bytes.

    RNS/Link.py:172#mode_from_lr_packet, RNS/Link.py:154#mtu_from_lr_packet,
    RNS/Link.py:234#Reticulum.MTU


Link id

The link id addresses every later packet on the link. It is a
SHA-256 truncated to 16 bytes, taken over

    (flags & 0x0f) || hops-less remainder of the packet

that is, the packet with the four high flag bits cleared and the hop
count removed, so that the id survives a hop; and, for a packet in
transport, with the transport id removed as well.

    RNS/Packet.py:348#get_hashable_part

Any signalling bytes are then chopped off the end. The initiator and
the responder therefore agree on the link id whether or not the MTU
was signalled, and a request that signals nothing opens the same link
as the same request that signals 500 bytes. The vectors
test/linkrequest/signalling and test/linkrequest/no-signalling carry
the same link id for exactly this reason.

test/linkrequest/in-transport carries it a third time, from a header 2
packet at one hop. Removing the transport id is part of the same rule
and is the half of it no other vector reaches: a request that arrived
through a transport node must open the link its sender opened, or the
two ends address different links from the first packet onward.

    RNS/Link.py:336#link_id_from_lr_packet

The link id is not a hash of the public keys, and it is not derived
from the destination hash alone. Two requests to the same destination
with the same keys but a different hop count produce the same id;
two requests with different keys do not.


Link request proof

Packet type 3, context 0xff. Two fields of the header depart from
every other packet in the corpus.

The destination type in the flags byte is set to link, 3, regardless
of the destination the proof is about, and it is written from a
constant rather than read from the destination.

    RNS/Packet.py:170#LRPROOF

The address field holds the link id, not a destination hash. It is
written from destination.link_id, and the payload is not encrypted.

    RNS/Packet.py:183#LRPROOF

    offset  size  field
    0       64    Ed25519 signature
    64      32    responder X25519 public key
    96      3     signalling, optional

The responder's Ed25519 key is not transmitted, and the proof carries
no identity. Only the X25519 half of the responder's link keypair is
sent, because that is all the initiator needs for the key agreement.

Two payload lengths decode, 96 and 99. The reference emits only the
99-byte form: prove always signals. Any other length is rejected as
invalid-length with both accepted lengths beside it;
test/linkproof/invalid-length is one byte short of the shorter. They
are not the two a link request accepts, which are 64 and 67: a proof
carries a signature where a request carries an Ed25519 key.

    RNS/Link.py:366#prove, RNS/Link.py:399#LINK_MTU_SIZE


Signed material

    link id || responder X25519 public || signer Ed25519 public
             || signalling

Three of the four parts are not in the packet.

    link id            derived from the link request
    signer Ed25519     the Ed25519 half of the identity of the
                       destination the request was addressed to
    signalling         present in the packet, and signed in the
                       position it occupies there

The signature is made with the identity key of the destination, not
with a key drawn for the link. That is not a rule about link request
proofs; it is a rule about the responder. Link.__init__ gives the
initiator a fresh Ed25519 key and gives the responder the identity key
of the destination it owns, so the responder signs everything with the
identity and the initiator signs everything with the link key. See the
section on delivery proofs below.

    RNS/Link.py:368#signed_data, RNS/Link.py:412#signed_data,
    RNS/Link.py:407#peer_sig_pub_bytes

An initiator that verifies against a key it did not already hold
proves nothing. The identity comes from an announce received earlier,
which is what binds a link to a destination.

test/linkproof/signalling-removed is the 96-byte form, made by
removing the signalling bytes from a valid proof. It decodes, and the
signature does not verify: the bytes were part of the signed material.


Key derivation

    shared  = X25519(own private, peer public)
    derived = HKDF-SHA256(length, shared, salt = link id, context = "")

The length is 64 bytes for MODE_AES256_CBC and 32 for
MODE_AES128_CBC, which is not enabled at this pin. The 64 bytes split
into a 32-byte signing key and a 32-byte encryption key, exactly as
doc/encryption describes for a token.

    RNS/Link.py:348#handshake, RNS/Link.py:607#get_salt,
    RNS/Link.py:610#get_context

The salt is the link id. It is not the identity hash, which is what a
packet to a destination uses, and it is in no packet: a decoder that
did not follow the request cannot derive the key even holding both
private keys.

The context is empty. get_context returns nothing, and hkdf turns
that into an empty string rather than refusing it.

    RNS/Cryptography/HKDF.py:50#context


Packets on the link

Destination type link, address field the link id.

    offset  size  field
    0       16    initialisation vector
    16      -     ciphertext, a multiple of 16 bytes
    -32     32    HMAC-SHA256

There is no ephemeral public key. Both ends already hold the shared
secret, so the payload is a bare token, 48 bytes of overhead rather
than 80. A payload shorter than that is not a token and is rejected as
short-payload; test/linkdata/short-payload is one byte short of it.

That rejection is the only one in the corpus that happens after the
header was printed. Whether a link data packet carries a token at all
is decided by the context byte, which is in the header, so the header
is read and printed first and the length rule applies to what is left.
The vector is decode class all the same: the payload it stopped at is
not in expect.

The context byte says what the plaintext holds. One context is not
encrypted at all:

    0x00   none            application data, and delivery proofs
    0x09   request         a msgpack array of three
    0x0a   response        a msgpack array of two
    0x0e   channel         a channel envelope
    0xfa   keepalive       one byte, in clear, not a token: 0xff from
                           the initiator and 0xfe in reply
    0xfb   link identify   identity public key || signature
    0xfc   link close      the link id
    0xfe   link rtt        msgpack float, sent by the initiator

    RNS/Packet.py:206#KEEPALIVE, RNS/Link.py:800#keepalive_packet,
    RNS/Link.py:1126#keepalive_packet

A decoder that treats every packet on a link as encrypted fails on
keepalives, and on delivery proofs.

0xfd is defined as LINKPROOF and no packet carries it. Nothing in the
reference sets that context, and the corpus has no vector for it
because there is nothing to record.

    RNS/Packet.py:90#LINKPROOF


Delivery proofs on a link

Packet type 3, destination type link, context 0x00, not encrypted. The
address field is the link id, the same as every other packet on the
link, and not the hash of the packet being proved; that is the whole
difference from a delivery proof to a destination, which doc/packet
describes.

    offset  size  field
    0       32    the hash of the packet being proved
    32      64    Ed25519 signature over that hash

Always the explicit form. prove_packet has the implicit branch
commented out, so 64 bytes never appears on a link even though
PacketReceipt would read it.

    RNS/Link.py:378#prove_packet

The key that signs is not the same at the two ends:

    initiator   the Ed25519 key it drew for this link, whose public
                half is in the link request
    responder   the identity key of the destination, whose public half
                is in an announce

    RNS/Link.py:276#X25519PrivateKey, RNS/Link.py:284#X25519PrivateKey

Nothing in a proof says which. A receiver knows because it knows which
end it is: each holds one peer_sig_pub, set when the link was
established, and verifies everything against it.

    RNS/Link.py:326#load_peer, RNS/Link.py:407#peer_sig_pub_bytes

test/proof/link-initiator and test/proof/link-responder are the two
directions over one link. An implementation that verifies both against
the same key passes neither, and one that verifies them against the
wrong key each passes neither; they are two vectors because one of them
alone could be passed by an implementation that always reaches for the
identity.


Request and response

Contexts 0x09 and 0x0a, and the first place msgpack is part of the wire
format rather than of an application above it. Both plaintexts are one
msgpack array and nothing else: no length, no type byte, no framing of
their own.

A request is three elements:

    0    float64   the sender's clock, as time.time returns it
    1    bin       16 bytes, SHA-256 of the path, truncated
    2    bin       the request body, or nil

    RNS/Link.py:487#request_path_hash

The path is not sent. Only its hash is, so the responder matches
against hashes it has registered and a path it has not registered is
indistinguishable from one that does not exist.

A response is two elements:

    0    bin       16 bytes, the request id
    1    bin       the response body

    RNS/Link.py:849#packed_response

The request id is the truncated hash of the request *packet*, taken by
the receiver, not a hash of the packed request. So it depends on the
header the request arrived with, and a decoder that hashes the payload
instead gets sixteen different bytes that look equally plausible.

    RNS/Link.py:989#getTruncatedHash

test/linkdata/request and test/linkdata/response are the two halves of
one exchange: the id in the second is the truncated packet hash of the
first.

The float is where implementations part company. umsgpack writes a
Python float as float64 always, so the eight bytes are an IEEE 754
double, big endian, and an implementation whose serialiser narrows to
float32, or emits an integer for a whole number, writes a packet the
reference reads as a different type. The corpus records the eight bytes
rather than a decimal, because that is what the wire carries.

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py:457#_pack2

nil and a zero-length byte string are different on the wire, 0xc0
against 0xc4 0x00, and this format spells both "-". Only the nil case
has a vector; a request with no data is what Link.request produces when
it is called with a path alone.


Channel

Context 0x0e. The plaintext is six bytes of header and then the
message, all three fields big endian:

    offset  size  field
    0       2     message type
    2       2     sequence
    4       2     declared message length
    6       -     the message

    RNS/Channel.py:133#MSGTYPE

The sequence counts messages sent, modulo 65536, and is what the
receiver orders and deduplicates by. It belongs to the channel and not
to the link: nothing in the link's own packets carries one.

The declared length is written and never read. Envelope.unpack takes
everything after offset 6 and does not compare it against the field, so
a message whose length field disagrees with its length is delivered
whole.

    RNS/Channel.py:118#unpack

The corpus carries the two vectors the reference can produce and none
where the two disagree, because it cannot produce one: Envelope.pack
writes the length from the message it packed. A decoder that truncates
to the declared length is therefore indistinguishable here and wrong on
the network.

The message type is the message's own, not the channel's. 0xff00 is
reserved for the stream messages RNS/Buffer.py sends; everything below
0xf000 belongs to whatever registered it.

    RNS/Channel.py:267#ME_INVALID_MSG_TYPE

test/linkdata/channel and test/linkdata/channel-eof carry two stream
messages, sequence 0 and 1. What is inside them is the message type's
business and is not decomposed here.


Identify

Sent by the initiator only, over the established link.

    plaintext   identity public key (64) || signature (64)
    signed      link id || identity public key

    RNS/Link.py:454#identify

The signature covers the link id, so an identify proof cannot be
lifted onto another link. Nothing else on a link names either end: the
keys in the request and the proof are per link, and the identity of
the initiator is revealed here or not at all.


Vector format

kind: linkrequest, determinism: encode

raw is one line:

    line 1    the complete packet

kind: linkproof, determinism: encode

raw is three lines:

    line 1    the link request this proof answers
    line 2    the public key of the identity that signed, 64 bytes
    line 3    the complete packet

kind: linkdata, determinism: encode

raw is three lines:

    line 1    the link request that opened the link
    line 2    the responder's link private key, 32 bytes
    line 3    the complete packet

Two of the three kinds need a packet other than the one they describe.
The link id, and with it the salt of the key derivation, is in no
packet on the link, and the responder's Ed25519 key is in no packet at
all. A decoder that ignores line 1 cannot produce a correct field for
either kind.

expect opens with those input lines, as link_request and either
signer_public or responder_private, so that it holds every byte of raw.
test/linkrequest/invalid-length is decode class; every other link
vector is encode class and cmd/check rebuilds all three lines.
